HyperTrace cross-guest packet provenance: remaining integrity contract
=====================================================================

Current boundary (2026-10-15)
-----------------------------

The guest creates a 32-byte mbufid_t after receiving a VirtIO DTrace VMID.
VirtIO-net passes that ID to bhyve, which checks only its canonical shape.
Tagged TAP accepts any canonical ID and stores it in a persistent mbuf tag.
The tag survives ordinary packet forwarding. The integrated sidecar change
restores MSIZE=256, and its allocation outcomes have DTrace probes, but no
host component proves that the guest was entitled to assert an origin.

A TX rule requiring mid_hostid to equal the sending VMID is insufficient:
guest B may legitimately forward a packet originated by guest A while
retaining A's ID. It would also leave msgid free for a guest to forge. A
kernel-side verifier must distinguish a fresh locally originated claim from
a relayed claim previously stamped by the host.

Required authenticated V2 contract
----------------------------------

The integrated branch has the V1-neutral foundation for item 1:
TAPSBINDVM takes a capability-limited VMM descriptor, checks its current
VMID and epoch through VM_GET_ATTESTATION, and stores the binding only on
an open, untagged TAP endpoint. bhyve binds before each tag enable, and
the binding is cleared on tag disable or close. An already tagged endpoint
can only confirm the same VMID and epoch; a changed incarnation fails the
restore path rather than relabeling queued packets. This does not
authenticate V1 IDs or implement any part of the V2 packet stamp. A
successful restore to a new incarnation still needs a quiesced V2 rebind.

1. A tagged TAP endpoint is bound to the exact VM incarnation through a
   read-only VMM attestation descriptor. libvmmapi already exports a
   CAP_IOCTL-only descriptor limited to VM_GET_ATTESTATION. The TAP binding
   must revalidate the descriptor at setup, reject zero or out-of-range
   VMIDs and epochs, and clear the binding when tagging is disabled or the
   endpoint closes. The bhyve TAP capability ioctl allowlist must include
   the new binding operation.

2. A versioned packet-ID wire format carries the source incarnation epoch
   and a host integrity stamp as well as hostid and msgid. V1 has only eight
   zero-required padding bytes and cannot carry both a full epoch and a
   strong stamp. V2 must have distinct VirtIO control negotiation and TAP mode;
   silently interpreting V1 padding as authority is forbidden. The packet
   ID remains an optional persistent mbuf tag; no bytes return to every
   mbuf header.

   The branch now reserves a 48-byte V2 shape: the existing 32-byte
   canonical ID, an eight-byte source epoch, and an eight-byte stamp.
   Empty records have all fields zero; a fresh guest claim has a canonical
   ID with zero epoch and stamp; a stamped record has both fields nonzero.
   Shape validation does not authenticate a stamp. A distinct command value
   and magic string let the guest and bhyve negotiate V2 ahead of V1.
   TAP has a separate V2 mode that requires a current bound VM
   incarnation. The candidate kernel computes a
   SipHash-2-4 stamp over a domain prefix and the first 40 record bytes,
   with a per-boot secret key. Epoch and stamp fields use little-endian
   encoding. The kernel TAP ingress policy checks that a guest stamp's
   source incarnation remains live.

3. On TAP ingress from a bound VM, an unstamped ID is accepted as a fresh
   origin only when its claimed VMID and epoch match that binding. The host
   stamps it using a kernel-held per-boot secret. A stamped ID from another
   origin is accepted only if the stamp verifies; this permits forwarding
   without letting a guest invent an unseen foreign origin. No host secret
   or unrestricted signing oracle is exposed to bhyve or a guest.

4. TAP egress preserves the stamp. VirtIO-net RX passes it to the guest
   without claiming that a canonical V1 ID is authenticated. The guest's
   persistent mbuf tag retains it across forwarding, and VirtIO-net TX
   returns it unmodified. Source trace correlation uses the visible origin
   fields; the new stamp is an integrity check, not a new origin.

5. Replayed observed IDs can still be copied by a compromised relay. The
   contract authenticates the claimed origin and incarnation, not the
   current packet payload or a guest's transformation. Document and test
   this limit rather than describing the stamp as content authenticity.

Required qualification
----------------------

The kernel now has an optional persistent 56-byte V2 mbuf tag (48-byte wire
record plus an eight-byte guest-local incarnation epoch), per-boot
stamp and constant-time verification functions, and a lock-free current-VM
epoch table. HyperTrace publishes and revokes exact VM incarnations at
allocation and release. Source-extracted native tests exercise actual
stamp code, tampering, relay preservation, host-origin creation, tag
upgrade and OOM preservation. TAP V2 ingress rejects unstamped foreign
claims and bad or revoked stamps, accepts a stamped relay from a live VM,
and stamps a fresh claim only for its bound current VM. The guest now tries
V2 first and bhyve requests TAP V2 mode when available. Snapshot format 4
records the mode and rejects nonempty staged V2 records during save or
restore. These paths compile and pass 44 native port suites, but ordinary
traffic has not been qualified in a live VM. Per-boot keys also mean
cross-host migration of already-stamped packets needs a secure host-to-host
   handoff or an explicit provenance reset before promotion. The guest now
   implements that reset for saved mbuf tags: an optional eight-byte local
   identity epoch accompanies the 48-byte wire record in the V2 persistent
   tag. VirtIO-DTrace updates the epoch when the guest identity changes;
   VirtIO-net preserves a stamped tag only if its local epoch is current,
   otherwise it starts a fresh local claim. No bytes are added to ordinary
   mbufs or to the V2 wire record. Live restore still needs to prove the
   rekey arrives and is applied before sustained packet transmission.

Host-origin V2 IDs now use a random per-boot ID outside the VMID namespace;
they do not depend on the guest-only DTrace node ID. Verification checks the
origin ID before accepting the host epoch, so a guest incarnation whose epoch
numerically matches the host epoch still becomes stale when its VMID is
revoked. TAP records an all-zero V2 ID in a persistent sidecar on ingress;
another TAP therefore forwards that guest's unknown origin as empty. Only an
mbuf with no V2 sidecar receives a new host-origin ID on TAP egress. Failure
to allocate the empty sidecar drops the packet rather than misattributing it.
V1 TAP ingress likewise retains an explicit empty sidecar, and a V1 sidecar
entering a V2 TAP is treated as unknown rather than authenticated host origin.
VirtIO-net now keeps an empty V2 receive record in a guest sidecar and sends
it onward as empty, so a guest relay does not invent its own origin for that
packet. Source-extracted stamp, TAP ingress, TAP egress, and guest VirtIO-net
tests cover these cases.

Reject foreign unstamped IDs, altered stamps, wrong epochs, old feature
versions, unbound TAP writers, and malformed empty IDs. Exercise a real
A-to-B-to-C relay and a forged A ID from B. Check VMID reuse across source
shutdown and restore. Compare throughput and allocation/failure rates for
tagging off, V1, and authenticated V2 on the same workload; use the
mbufid-sidecar script only for allocation counts, not as a throughput
benchmark. The current host lacks /dev/vmm and /dev/dtrace/dtrace, so none
of the live checks has passed. Do not promote this branch on the basis
of this design or the current component tests.
